  "year": "1984",
  "subtitle": "The first dedicated, non-destructive hard-disk audio editing workstation — scrub the jog/shuttle wheel through a sound you can cut, copy, and reorder without ever destroying the original.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The AudioFile changed how recorded sound is edited. Instead of splicing magnetic tape, it stored audio on hard disks and let the engineer jog and shuttle a sound through a waveform display, marking edits and rearranging them into a non-destructive edit decision list (EDL) that played back in real time. The console's jog/shuttle wheel and tall rack of labelled keys are the physical grammar of a whole new creative interaction: time becomes a thing you can scrub, zoom, and reorder.",
  "search_terms": ["AMS AudioFile", "Advanced Music Systems AudioFile", "AMS 10 AudioFile", "non-destructive audio editing", "digital audio workstation history", "Stuart Nevison", "jog shuttle wheel editor"],
  "research": {
